      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "This problem involves the concepts of factorials and pattern recognition. Factorials hide patterns in them; understanding the patterns may help in solving problems involving factorials. \n$$\n\\begin{array}{llllll} \n1! =0\\text{ zeroes}\\\\ \n2! = 2\\text{ zeroes} &\\\\\n3! = 6\\text{ zeroes} &\\\\\n4! =24\\text{ zeroes} &\\\\\n5! = 120\\text{ zeroes}\\\\\n6! =720\\text{ zeroes} &\\\\\n7! =5040\\text{ zeroes}\\\\\n8! =40320\\text{ zeroes} &\\\\\n9! =362880\\text{ zeroes} &\\\\\n\\end{array}\n$$\nWe can see that $n = 5$ is the largest multiple of $3$ in this sequence. If we increase $m$ by $1$, the pattern will occur again.\nHowever, we now need to solve for the next largest multiple of $3$. To get to the next highest multiple of $3$, we need to add $2$ to the current number. $m = 9+2n=9+2*5$. Since $m=9+2*5$ is much bigger than $1992$ what may happen? There are many other possibilities as well. For example, $1992=13*152$ and one is not a factorial tail then the other one is not a factorial tail. \nBy searching reverse for the above constraints, we are able to determine the largest value of $n$ such that there will be many solutions.\nThere are $1, 2, 4, 9\\cdots 5n$. Among them, we only have multiple of $3$ now, so the largest is $16$. So the answer is $16-1=15$.\nWe can also solve this problem by using a different method. The number of trailing zeroes in $m!$ is:\n$$\nZ(m)=\\left\\lfloor \\frac{m}{5} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{m}{25} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{m}{125} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots\n$$\nwith:\n$$\n\\begin{align}\n&\\{ \\frac {n}{5} \\}= n, \\text{ remounted mod 5} \\\\\n&\\{ \\frac {n}{25} \\}= n, \\text{ remounted mod 25}\\\\\n&\\vdots \\\\\n&\\end{align}\n$$\nAnd calculate how many numbers we can deal with.\n<iframe width=\"560\" height=\"315\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/DpeVZTmLlqM\" title=\"YouTube video player\" frameborder=\"0\" allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture\" allowfullscreen></iframe>\nIn the context of factorials, trailing zeroes can be described as the number of times a factor of 5 in the expansion of the factorial is cancelled out by factors of 2. Clearly, the number of tiers of 5's that cancel out the number of tiers of 2s is less than or equal to the number of tiers of 2s. Thus, our mission is to count how many tiers of 5s can be counted (as facr2 = (facr* facr/5)), progressively adding 5's such that our facr2 score >= facr\n\n\nsee the other question for proof: https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c4h1118238_factorials_with_multiples_of_5_arcsin_1445060 of julian_nathan",
